== 4. Reading this ============================================= 
  If most substantial-count cells are flagged, the melanocyte rate is 
  effectively zero and the manuscript can say RASSF9 is not expressed 
  in normal melanocytes, with doublets named as the source of the few 
  positives. 
 
  If they are NOT flagged -- keratin-low with ordinary gene counts -- 
  then a rare population of normal melanocytes does express RASSF9, 
  at roughly 1 in 1,000. That is worth one honest sentence rather 
  than a claim of absence, and it would sit comfortably with an 
  induction model: a gene off at baseline in nearly every cell, with 
  a few cells already carrying it. 
 
  WHAT THIS CANNOT DO: a count matrix cannot prove a cell is a 
  singlet. Flagging is evidence of a doublet; absence of a flag is 
  not proof of a single melanocyte. Neither reading should be stated 
  more strongly than that. 
